  "notes": "Maximalist 1980s synth-pop power-ballad and camp dance-pop, and one of the fully documented entries in this section: key, tempo, range, instrument-by-instrument credits and the writing team are all sourced. The drums are synth-generated by the entry's own account — \"gated 1980s snare on 2&4, four-square kick, and a pulsing 8th-note synth acting as the hat; live-feel percussion is minimal — the groove is synth-generated\" — so the snare carries a large reverb tail and a hard transient, `hihat.base` is 8th with no roll vocabulary, and the perc lanes shrink to two at a low density. \"Drums enter at the chorus and often drop entirely for the bridge\" is the arrangement's deep drop-out. The bass is explicitly not an 808: \"a pulsing 8th-note synth bass locked to the kick is the engine\", which is `mirror_kick` at 0.96 with repeated-root cell shapes and a near-zero glide. Harmony is \"4-bar diatonic loops with a borrowed minor iv for the pre-chorus\" and is \"functional and cadence-driven rather than modal\", so the families are cadential, `triad` is high and the borrowed iv is the `borrowed` map rather than a separate family. Key is the documented D major with the belted top around F♯5 inside an A3—F♯5 working range, which is the melody register exactly; \"toplines start in a low speaking register and climb by 3rds and 5ths to a sustained belt\" is the arch contour, the third-weighted intervals and `long_arc`. \"Deliberate tempo drift\" on the flagship single is the one place humanize takes a real number. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum probability, the humanize pair, the perc gain and `session.keys` beyond its first value; the research documents a palette and a range, not a pattern, and its key line reads only \"bright majors — the flagship is D major\", so A and G are inferred neighbouring bright majors and B minor is D's relative minor — none of the three is named in the entry. `bpm.mode` 117 is the entry's own stated flagship tempo, not an inference.",
